diff --git a/docs/implementation.md b/docs/implementation.md
index 4c0aad4..8f81a16 100644
--- a/docs/implementation.md
+++ b/docs/implementation.md
@@ -8,8 +8,9 @@
 
 ## Live games
 
-- Creating a game snapshots the quiz title, number of winners, and ordered active questions, including options and their correctness. Edits to the quiz cannot affect a game in progress. Player and answer records are scoped to the game; deleting the source quiz also deletes its games and their records.
+- Creating a game snapshots the quiz title, number of winners, timer duration, and ordered active questions, including options and their correctness. Edits to the quiz cannot affect a game in progress. Player and answer records are scoped to the game; deleting the source quiz also deletes its games and their records.
 - A game advances from waiting to open to closed for each round, then ends. The transition version prevents delayed moderator actions or answers from affecting the wrong phase or question. An answer upserts one row per player and round; the same transaction lock guards transitions and writes. When a question is open, the server omits correctness flags from every client payload, including the initial HTML.
+- A timed round stores its deadline when opening, and the answer transaction checks it before accepting a pick. A recurring task in Puma checks the indexed deadlines even with no browsers connected, including after a restart, and takes the same lock and close transition as manual Finish. Only moderator state includes remaining milliseconds; the projector renders M:SS with a monotonic browser clock. Transfer ignores the timer fields and imports start untimed.
 - Player identity is an encrypted, HTTP-only cookie scoped to one join URL. Each open tab renews a presence lease about every 10 seconds and reports departure on navigation where the browser permits it. A lost connection expires after 35 seconds. The live answer counter compares present players who answered with all present players; the result counts retain votes from players who left.
 - A purpose-scoped signed token lets the moderator and joined players subscribe to the game's Action Cable stream. After committing a round opening the server pushes its version and question text/options to all subscribers, so the phones can render it immediately without another request. Correctness, picks, and personal results are never sent over the shared stream. Other changes notify clients to refetch without delay; periodic HTTP reads cover reconnects and clients without WebSockets. The moderator also polls for live counts. The async adapter runs in the single Puma process started by `bin/serve`.
 - Each round stores its server-side opening time. Every valid upsert records the elapsed microseconds at acceptance, even when the option did not change. When ending, the game stores its complete leaderboard: one point per correct final pick, ascending total time of those correct picks as the first tiebreaker, and player ID as the stable final tiebreaker. All joined players are ranked, regardless of presence. Games from before this migration can still be scored, but older picks have no timing data.
